A||||6||GarethWright||Ball Joints on the front||||1||
Z||000000||GarethWright||01-11-2001||05:08 PM||||I need new upper and lower ball joints and inner tie rod ends on the front of my '87 GT.  According to Haynes I need a whole new Lower Control Arm, as the balljoint is welded to it.  The Fiero Store sells Just the Lower Ball Joint (p/n 57012) for $27.52 each.  My question is, can I get Just the ball joint and have a muffler shop weld it back on to the control arm, or Do I just bolt it on and not worry about the welding?  I read a previous post from the archives that said I could grind through the weld on the ball joint to get it off.  How do I press the steering knuckle into the new ball joint without damaging it?  <BR>Any other hints and tips would be really appreciated as this will be my first attempt at ball joints.<BR>Thanks in advance,<BR>Gareth<p>[This message has been edited by GarethWright (edited 01-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||01-11-2001||05:22 PM||||Garth,<P>The Haynes manual is wrong in this instance (they're wrong alot). If there ever is a weld (with the exception of some '84's) it's a spot. Your lower ball joints are press in, press out.<BR>The upper's (if stock) are installed with rivits that need to be drilled out. The replacements, which are adjustable, are bolt in.<BR>The inner tie-rod ends require a special tool to remove (some people use a pipe wrench?) which I 'borrow' from a local parts store that has a so called 'rental program' set-up.<BR>Go ahead and buy some! The local shops can install them for you or you can go out on a limb and do them yourself.<BR>The 'steering knuckle' tightens with the nut and has no-other worries.<BR>Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||01-11-2001||05:23 PM||||The lowers are not really welded in, they are pressed in. There is a small tack weld which is easily cut with a dremel or similar tool. The installation of the B joints  does require the use of a press, either portable or bench type. If you do not feel comfortable doing this job yourself just about any repair shop can do it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||01-11-2001||05:24 PM||||Resto: you type faster than me||||reg||1
